    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.snippets\n\n### Description\n\nHey there, first things first: Thanks for all your work! I am slowly going through all of mini's modules and evaluating if they can replace more complex stuff in my existing config, and I love the design and focus of it.\n\nBut mini.snippets is a tad to static for my taste, and I don't even use very complicated snippets. My most urgent problem is the insertion of dates in a specific format: `%Y-%m-%d %a`.\n\nSolutions I found so far:\n- Using a .lua snippets file and `os.date` for the body of the snippet. Works for a day (my editor usually runs for a week), and even that only because I need a date- and not a not timestamp.\n- Defining a dynamic lookup for a specific variable. Requires splitting part of my snippets into actual config files, I don't like this.\n- Using a different plugin and losing mini.completion integration along with it.\n\nMy preferred way would be to have the option to call Lua for a tabstop value (not LSP-standard, I know, but a useful extension to define, I think). That could even be written in a way that allows feeding other tab stop values into it (`function(_1, _2, _3, ...)`). Second best would be if the body key in a snippet table was allowed to be a function that gets called on expansion (`prefix = \"d\", body = function() ...`).\n\nI think the latter is both more complicated and less flexible than the former, but both would enable me to do a lot more with snippets. What do you think?\n",
