            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion and kind words!\n\nThis type of dynamic snippets is already supported by the facts that `MiniSnippets.config.snippets` is allowed to have function loaders (basically what is described as \"second best\" option) and that they can be [\"recursive\" with default prepare step](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/22619434ccca787cdb34406c97f0e395d4debadc/doc/mini-snippets.txt#L928-L929). In particular, its elements can be functions that return functions.\n\nSo the most straightforward way is to define a custom inline function loader directly in the `require('mini.snippets').setup()`:\n\n```lua\nrequire('mini.snippets').setup({\n  snippets = {\n    -- ... Other loaders ...\n\n    -- Custom loader that is not cached\n    function()\n      return { { prefix = 'date', body = vim.fn.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %a'), desc = 'Date %Y-%m-%d %a' } }\n    end,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> * Using a .lua snippets file and `os.date` for the body of the snippet. Works for a day (my editor usually runs for a week), and even that only because I need a date- and not a not timestamp.\n\nThe variation of this is also possible, but the output needs to be a function that is evaluated during expansion and not during `*.lua` file source. So with `~/.config/nvim/snippets/dynamic.lua` having this content:\n\n```lua\nreturn {\n  function()\n    return { prefix = 'date', body = vim.fn.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %a'), desc = 'Date %Y-%m-%d %a' }\n  end,\n}\n```\n\n... the following `setup()` will also work:\n\n```lua\nlocal gen_loader = require('mini.snippets').gen_loader\nrequire('mini.snippets').setup({\n  snippets = {\n    -- ... Other loaders\n\n    -- File loader that caches its output, but function items stay functions\n    gen_loader.from_file('~/.config/nvim/snippets/dynamic.lua'),\n  },\n})\n```\n---\n\nSo as far as I understand, this is already possible and works as expected.",
